[{"slug":"your-unique-terrain-roadmap","title":"Your Unique Terrain Roadmap","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Your Unique Terrain Roadmap\n\nYou are not a collection of isolated parts. Your gut, sleep, training, nutrition, stress,\nnervous system, immune function and metabolism all interact. Your programme maps this\nterrain and adapts step by step — not toward one fixed plan, but toward the plan that\nfits where you are right now.\n\n## What your terrain means\n\nYour terrain is your biological context. It explains why the same advice does not work\nthe same way for two people. You can eat the same meal, train the same session or sleep\nthe same hours as someone else and respond differently.\n\nYour terrain includes:\n\n- Nutrition and digestion\n- Training history and recovery capacity\n- Sleep and stress\n- Micronutrient status\n- Nervous-system state\n- Metabolism, immune load and inflammation\n- Daily habits\n\nThe better question is never *what is the best plan?* — it is **what is the best plan\nfor your current terrain?** And your terrain shifts with sleep, stress, travel, illness,\ntraining load and consistency.\n\n## The FunctionAlps performance map\n\nSeven connected systems feed your energy, metabolism, performance and recovery:\n\n1. **Nutrition and micronutrients** — macros, micros, hydration, food quality.\n2. **Gut and absorption** — digestion, tolerance, microbiome, nutrient uptake.\n3. **Strength and muscle-building** — mechanical signal, full-body training, lower-limb\n   capacity, eccentric work.\n4. **Cardio and energy systems** — Zone 2 base, Zones 4–5 ceiling, HIIT, walking.\n5. **Recovery** — biological, physical and nervous-system recovery.\n6. **Nervous system** — recruitment, coordination, breathing, stress regulation.\n7. **Immunity and inflammation** — immune load, inflammation tone, resilience.\n\n## How we use your roadmap\n\nStep 1 — identify your current priority. Step 2 — choose the signal that fits it:\nwalking for a low-fatigue aerobic base, HIIT for a strong metabolic push, strength for a\nmuscle-building stimulus, eccentric work for control and tissue capacity, protein for\nrebuilding, carbohydrates for refuelling, morning light for your circadian rhythm,\nbreathing or NSDR for the nervous system. Step 3 — use the minimum effective dose. Step\n4 — track your response: energy, sleep, digestion, recovery, mood, performance. Step 5 —\nadapt.\n\n**Map the terrain, choose the signal, track the response, adapt the plan.**\n\n## What to remember\n\nOnce a week, check your terrain:\n\n- Nutrition: enough, and nutrient-dense?\n- Gut: digesting and tolerating meals well?\n- Training: right signal, right progression?\n- Cardio: base work or ceiling work this week?\n- Recovery: refuelling, sleeping, downshifting?\n- Nervous system: regulated and ready?\n- Immunity: resilient, or running low?\n\nThen ask: *what improved, and what needs adjusting?*\n"},{"slug":"morning-wake-up-routine","title":"Morning Wake-Up Routine","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Morning Wake-Up Routine\n\nYour morning gives your body its first signals of the day: light, movement, breath and\nrhythm. A morning wake-up routine isn't a workout — it's a short activation sequence\nthat helps you start the day cleanly.\n\n## Why the morning matters\n\nYour body runs on rhythms — sleep/wake, hormones, temperature, appetite, alertness,\ndigestion, training readiness and energy. Morning light is a strong time signal: it\ntells your brain the day has started. Outdoor light is usually far stronger than indoor\nlight, even on cloudy days. Target 5–15 minutes outside in the morning when you can\n(never stare at the sun).\n\nMorning movement increases blood flow, joint lubrication, muscle activation, breathing\nand nervous-system readiness. The goal is activation, not fatigue.\n\n## What the routine should do\n\nA good morning routine should wake up your nervous system, restore movement after\nsleep, create energy without stress, and anchor your day. Morning stiffness is normal —\nuse gentle movement through range: spine, hips, shoulders, ankles, neck, rib cage and\nbreath.\n\nThink of it this way: your workout is the training stimulus, your wake-up routine is the\nactivation signal. Consistency matters more than intensity here.\n\n## Your morning wake-up routine\n\nA 6–10 minute structure: **light** (get outside or near bright natural light, 5–15\nminutes), **breathe** (60–90 seconds nasal breathing, longer exhale), **mobilise** (2–3\nminutes for spine, hips, shoulders, ankles), **activate** (2–3 minutes of glute bridges,\nsquats, incline push-ups, dead bugs, calf raises or band pull-aparts), and an **optional\nwalk** (5–10 minutes easy walking).\n\nAn 8-minute template: 1 min breathing, 2 min mobility, 3 min activation, 2 min easy walk\nor light movement.\n\n## What to remember\n\nFor two weeks: a 6–10 minute wake-up routine, at least 5 mornings a week, including\nlight, breathing, mobility, activation and an optional walk.\n\nTrack energy, mood, stiffness, focus, sleep quality, training readiness and how\nrepeatable it feels.\n"},{"slug":"feedback-and-self-tracking","title":"Feedback and Self-Tracking","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Feedback and Self-Tracking\n\nYour feedback is part of the programme. A plan only becomes personal to you when we\nunderstand how your body actually responds to each signal you're given.\n\n## Why feedback matters\n\nYour body doesn't respond the same way every day. The same workout can feel completely\ndifferent depending on sleep, stress, food, hydration, digestion, immune load and\nrecovery.\n\nWithout feedback, a plan is a theory. With feedback, it becomes alive.\n\nTracking is not about control, and it shouldn't make you obsessive — it should make you\nclearer. What matters are trends, not one-off events. Your subjective feedback matters as\nmuch as any wearable data: a watch can estimate sleep, heart rate, HRV and activity, but\nit can't understand your motivation, digestion, mental clarity, effort or confidence.\n\n## What to track\n\nThe most useful markers:\n\n1. **Energy** — morning, afternoon, after meals, after training, next day.\n2. **Sleep** — duration, quality, restfulness, timing, night waking.\n3. **Digestion** — bloating, heaviness, stool, appetite, tolerance.\n4. **Training performance** — loads, reps, effort, technique, confidence.\n5. **Recovery** — soreness, readiness, resting heart rate/HRV if available.\n6. **Mood and motivation** — clarity, irritability, drive, feeling flat or energised.\n\nThe question behind every entry: did this action give you energy, cost you energy, or\nstabilise it?\n\n## How to use your feedback\n\nThe weekly loop is: **action → response → interpretation → adjustment.**\n\n- **Green** — energised, sleep stable, soreness manageable, digestion good, performance\n  improving. Continue, or progress slowly.\n- **Yellow** — more tired, soreness lingering, sleep slightly disrupted, motivation\n  lower. Observe, and adjust slightly.\n- **Red** — sleep worsening, drained for days, performance dropping, excessive soreness,\n  digestion worsening, pain appearing. Reduce, modify or pause.\n\nWearables are one layer — don't obey them blindly. Combine the numbers with how you\nactually feel: symptoms, energy, digestion, soreness, stress and motivation.\n\n## What to remember\n\nEvery evening for two weeks, rate: energy (low / medium / high), sleep (poor / okay /\ngood), digestion (poor / okay / good), training or movement (done / partial / missed),\nrecovery (sore / normal / ready).\n\nThen answer one question: *what did I notice today?*\n"},{"slug":"signals-create-adaptation","title":"Signals Create Adaptation","tags":null,"body_md":"![Signals Create Adaptation infographic](https://ndojytvvlvlbgtodujkf.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/content-public/library/signals-create-adaptation/02_signals_create_adaptation.png)\n\n## The right challenge, repeated well, becomes progress\n\nOne of the most important ideas in your programme is that your body is constantly responding to signals.\n\nExercise is one of those signals.\n\nSo are:\n\n- walking;\n- food;\n- sleep;\n- stress;\n- light;\n- breathing;\n- recovery;\n- and the amount of energy available to you.\n\nYour body does not simply respond to what you *intend* to do.\n\nIt responds to what you repeatedly expose it to.\n\nThat is why consistency matters — but it is also why **dose and recovery matter**.\n\n## Training is a signal, not the adaptation itself\n\nWhen you exercise, you temporarily challenge the system.\n\nDepending on the type of training, that challenge can include:\n\n- mechanical tension in muscle and connective tissue;\n- increased energy demand;\n- higher oxygen demand;\n- metabolic stress;\n- nervous-system recruitment;\n- changes in circulation;\n- and temporary fatigue.\n\nThose changes tell the body:\n\n> “This level of demand exists. Be better prepared for it next time.”\n\nBut the workout itself is not the long-term adaptation.\n\nIt is the **stimulus**.\n\n## First comes the response\n\nAfter a signal, the body begins responding.\n\nThat response depends on the type and size of the challenge.\n\nFor example:\n\n- endurance work increases the demand for aerobic energy production;\n- strength work increases mechanical and neural demand;\n- higher-intensity intervals challenge oxygen delivery and high-output energy systems;\n- repeated movement increases demand for efficiency and work capacity.\n\nAt the cellular level, the body senses changes in:\n\n- energy availability;\n- mechanical load;\n- calcium signalling;\n- oxygen demand;\n- hormones;\n- nutrients;\n- and other local signals.\n\nYou do not need to memorize those pathways.\n\nThe important concept is:\n\n> **Your body detects the demand and starts preparing a response.**\n\n## Then recovery gives that response somewhere to go\n\nAfter training, the system needs enough resources and time to rebuild.\n\nRecovery includes:\n\n- sleep;\n- adequate energy;\n- protein;\n- carbohydrate replacement where appropriate;\n- hydration;\n- micronutrients;\n- lower-intensity movement;\n- nervous-system downregulation;\n- and enough time before the next large stressor.\n\nThis is when the training signal can begin turning into a more durable adaptation.\n\n## Repetition creates adaptation\n\nA single training session can make you tired.\n\nRepeated, appropriately dosed training can make you **more capable**.\n\nOver time, different signals can contribute to changes such as:\n\n- greater mitochondrial content and function;\n- improved aerobic capacity;\n- better blood supply to working muscle;\n- greater strength;\n- muscle growth;\n- improved motor-unit recruitment;\n- better movement efficiency;\n- stronger tissues;\n- and improved recovery between efforts.\n\nThe exact adaptation depends on the signal.\n\nThat is why we use different training tools for different goals.\n\n## The dose matters\n\nMore signal is not automatically better.\n\nThink of training dose in three zones.\n\n### Too little\n\nThe body receives very little reason to change.\n\nResult:\n\n**little adaptation.**\n\n### Appropriate challenge\n\nThe session is demanding enough to create a useful signal, but manageable enough that you can recover and repeat high-quality work.\n\nResult:\n\n**progressive adaptation.**\n\n### Too much for your current capacity\n\nThe stress is larger than the recovery available.\n\nOne difficult session is not automatically harmful, but repeatedly exceeding your ability to recover can reduce the quality of the training process.\n\nResult:\n\n**fatigue can accumulate faster than useful adaptation.**\n\nThe target is therefore not maximal suffering.\n\nThe target is the **smallest useful dose that can be progressed over time**.\n\n## The same session is not the same signal for everybody\n\nTen minutes of hard cycling may be a manageable stimulus for one person and an enormous stressor for another.\n\nThe same applies to:\n\n- strength volume;\n- running;\n- squats;\n- fasting;\n- caloric restriction;\n- sleep loss;\n- work stress;\n- or a busy week.\n\nYour current capacity changes the meaning of the signal.\n\nThis is why your programme is not built around rigid universal rules.\n\nWe introduce a signal.\n\nWe observe the response.\n\nThen we decide whether to:\n\n- repeat it;\n- progress it;\n- reduce it;\n- change it;\n- or recover before adding more.\n\n## What should you notice?\n\nYou do not need to measure everything.\n\nA few simple markers can tell us whether the dose is working.\n\nUseful observations include:\n\n- How was your energy afterwards?\n- How did you sleep?\n- How sore were you?\n- How quickly did your breathing settle?\n- Did the next session feel better or worse?\n- Was your motivation stable?\n- Did you feel ready to train again?\n- Was your digestion affected?\n- Did performance improve over several exposures?\n\nOne individual day does not tell the whole story.\n\nWe are looking for **patterns**.\n\n## The principle for the rest of your programme\n\nEvery phase that follows uses the same logic.\n\nZone 2 is a signal.\n\nHIIT is a signal.\n\nStrength training is a signal.\n\nEccentric work is a signal.\n\nFood is a signal and a source of material.\n\nSleep and recovery influence how the body responds to all of them.\n\nThe programme therefore follows:\n\n```text\nSIGNAL\n↓\nRESPONSE\n↓\nRECOVERY\n↓\nADAPTATION\n↓\nGREATER CAPACITY\n```\n\nThe goal is not to collect difficult workouts.\n\nThe goal is to progressively build a body that can **do more, recover better, and tolerate more over time**.\n\n## Key takeaway\n\n> **We are not chasing exhaustion. We are choosing the right signal, at the right dose, and giving your body the opportunity to adapt.**"},{"slug":"strength-is-more-than-muscle-size","title":"Strength Is More Than Muscle Size","tags":null,"body_md":"## Your nervous system determines how much strength you can access\n\nMuscle produces force, but muscle does not act alone.\n\nEvery voluntary movement begins with the nervous system.\n\nThe brain organizes the movement. Signals travel through the spinal cord and peripheral nerves. Motor neurons activate muscle fibres.\n\nThe nervous system influences:\n- which motor units are recruited;\n- how many are recruited;\n- how quickly they fire;\n- and how well different muscles coordinate.\n\nThis means strength is not only about having more muscle.\n\nIt is also about learning to use the muscle you already have more efficiently.\n\n![Neural recruitment — brain, spinal cord, motor nerve, neuromuscular junction, motor units, force](https://ndojytvvlvlbgtodujkf.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/content-public/library/strength-is-more-than-muscle-size/03_brain_nerves_muscle_recruitment.png)\n\n## Practice improves recruitment and coordination\n\nWhen a movement is unfamiliar, the nervous system may be less efficient.\n\nYou may experience:\n- poor timing;\n- unnecessary co-contraction;\n- lower confidence;\n- inefficient force production;\n- or the feeling that a movement is disproportionately difficult.\n\nRepeated quality practice can improve:\n- coordination;\n- timing;\n- motor-unit recruitment;\n- movement confidence;\n- and accessible force.\n\nThis is one reason we do not interpret every difficult movement simply as “you need bigger muscles.”\n\nSometimes the system needs practice, control and progressively appropriate loading.\n\n## Eccentric training: strength through the lowering phase\n\nThe lowering phase of an exercise is called the **eccentric** phase.\n\nDuring an eccentric contraction, muscle produces force while lengthening.\n\nExamples include:\n- lowering a weight;\n- descending into a squat;\n- lowering from a calf raise;\n- controlling the descent of a row or press;\n- slowing the body during landing or deceleration.\n\nThe lowering phase is not passive. It requires active force production and control.\n\nEccentric work can provide a strong mechanical signal while also developing the ability to absorb and control force.\n\n![Eccentric training — controlled lowering, force absorption and gradual progression](https://ndojytvvlvlbgtodujkf.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/content-public/library/strength-is-more-than-muscle-size/04_eccentric_training_strength_through_lowering_phase.png)\n\n## Progress eccentric work gradually\n\nEccentric training can produce substantial delayed soreness when it is unfamiliar.\n\nTherefore:\n- begin with one or two selected exercises;\n- use controlled lowering;\n- avoid dramatically increasing volume at the same time;\n- monitor soreness and recovery;\n- progress only when the response is appropriate.\n\nThe goal is not to make every exercise slow. The goal is to use eccentric work strategically.\n\n## Strength through range\n\nWe also want to move beyond stretching alone.\n\nA useful long-term goal is:\n\n> **Build strength in the ranges you want to use.**\n\nLoaded range-of-motion work means taking a movement through an appropriate range while maintaining control and some form of resistance.\n\nThis can help combine:\n- mobility;\n- strength;\n- movement confidence;\n- and tissue tolerance.\n\nExamples may eventually include:\n- controlled split squats;\n- goblet squats where appropriate;\n- loaded hinges;\n- overhead range work;\n- calf work through full range;\n- and other exercises selected together at your review.\n\n## Lower body: information, not a diagnosis\n\nYou have mentioned that squats feel strenuous.\n\nWe should treat that as information.\n\nIt does not automatically mean:\n- squats are unsafe;\n- the lower body should be avoided;\n- or a barbell back squat is required.\n\nPotential options include:\n- goblet squats;\n- split squats;\n- supported squat patterns;\n- adjusted range;\n- controlled eccentric work;\n- gradual loading.\n\nThis should remain a later, assessment-dependent subsection.\n\n## Key takeaway\n\n> **Strength is what your muscles and nervous system can produce together. Practice, recruitment, control, eccentric strength and usable range all help turn muscle into real physical capacity.**"},{"slug":"breathing-exercises","title":"Breathing Exercises","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Breathing Exercises\n\nBreathing is one of the fastest ways to communicate with your nervous system. You can't\ndirectly tell your heart rate or stress chemistry to calm down — but you can change your\nbreathing, and your body receives a different signal.\n\n## Why breathing matters\n\nBreathing is linked to oxygen, carbon dioxide, heart-rate patterns, stress state,\nposture, focus and recovery. Your state changes your breathing — but your breathing can\nalso change your state. Fast, shallow breathing tends to signal activation; slow,\ncontrolled breathing tends to signal downregulation, and longer exhales are often\nespecially useful for calm.\n\nThe goal isn't to become a breathwork expert. The goal is to use breathing as a state\ntool.\n\n## What happens in the body\n\nDifferent breathing tools create different effects. **Slow breathing** is useful for\ndownregulation — often around 4–6 breaths per minute, though comfort matters more than\nhitting a perfect number. **Cyclic sighing** (a double inhale plus a long exhale) is\nuseful for acute stress and a quick reset. **Box breathing** (inhale, hold, exhale,\nhold) is useful for focus and composure, but isn't ideal if breath-holds feel stressful\nto you.\n\nAfter hard effort, breathing can bridge activation and recovery — use it to come down\nafter HIIT, intense strength work or work stress.\n\n## How to use breathing exercises\n\nThree starter protocols:\n\n1. **Extended exhale** — inhale 4 sec, exhale 6 sec, for 3–5 minutes. Best for calming,\n   evenings, after training, before sleep.\n2. **Cyclic sighing** — inhale through the nose, a second small inhale on top, then a\n   long slow exhale. Repeat for 1–3 minutes. Best for a quick reset and releasing\n   tension.\n3. **Box breathing** — 4 sec inhale, 4 sec hold, 4 sec exhale, 4 sec hold. Repeat for\n   2–4 minutes. Best for focus and composure.\n\nUse breathing in the morning, before training, after training, during work stress,\nbefore sleep, or whenever pressure rises.\n\n## What to remember\n\nFor two weeks, test all three: extended exhale for 3–5 min after training or before\nsleep, cyclic sighing for 1–3 min when stress rises, box breathing for 2–4 min before\nfocus work or training.\n\nTrack your calm, focus, heart-rate recovery, sleep and stress — and notice which\nprotocol feels best for you.\n"},{"slug":"macros-vs-micros","title":"Macros vs Micros","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Macros vs Micros\n\nMacros are the foundation. Micros are the machinery. Calories, protein, carbohydrates\nand fats provide fuel and structure; vitamins, minerals and cofactors help your body use\nthat fuel and run the processes behind performance.\n\n## What macros do\n\nMacronutrients are needed in larger amounts. **Protein** provides amino acids for tissue\nrepair, muscle-building, enzymes and immune function. **Carbohydrates** are fast fuel and\nrefill muscle glycogen for training, high-intensity work and recovery. **Fats** support\ncell membranes, hormone physiology, brain health and the absorption of fat-soluble\nvitamins.\n\nFor muscle gain, macros answer three questions: are you eating enough total energy, are\nyou getting enough protein, and are carbohydrates and fats matched to your training,\ndigestion and goals? If yes, we go deeper into micronutrients.\n\n## What micros do\n\nMicronutrients are needed in smaller amounts, but their role is huge — they help turn\nfood into energy, training into adaptation and recovery into progress. Examples:\nmagnesium for ATP-related energy production and nerve/muscle function, B vitamins for\nenergy pathways and red blood cell synthesis, zinc for immune function and repair,\nvitamin D for bone, muscle and immune function, omega-3 for cell membranes and\ninflammatory balance, choline for brain, liver and cell membranes.\n\nMicronutrients don't replace calories or protein — they support the system that uses\nthem.\n\n## Why food quality matters\n\nYou can hit your macros with low-quality or repetitive food and still miss important\nnutrients — commonly magnesium, zinc, selenium, calcium, iron, omega-3, vitamin D, B12,\ncholine, folate, potassium and iodine.\n\nNutrient-dense foods help close those gaps: eggs, sardines, oysters, liver, dairy, meat,\nseafood, colourful plants, legumes, nuts and seeds. The goal isn't obsession — it's\nmaking sure your body has the fuel and tools it needs to adapt.\n\n## What to remember\n\nKeep your macros stable, then ask daily: did I eat protein at each main meal, did I\ninclude at least one nutrient-dense food, did I include colourful plants, did I include\nan omega-3-rich food this week, did I repeat the same meals too often, and did digestion\nfeel good?\n"},{"slug":"biological-recovery","title":"Biological Recovery","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Biological Recovery\n\nYour body cannot rebuild from nothing. Training creates the signal; biological recovery\nprovides the raw materials — protein, carbohydrates, energy, micronutrients, fats,\nhydration, electrolytes and sleep.\n\n## Protein: the rebuilding signal\n\nProtein provides amino acids, which repair tissue, build muscle and support enzymes,\nimmune function and many structural processes. Leucine matters especially, because it\nhelps switch on muscle protein synthesis.\n\nPractical target: 20–40 g of high-quality protein per meal, distributed across the day —\nespecially useful after training if your last protein meal was several hours earlier.\nProtein timing matters, but it's not magic; total daily protein and distribution matter\nfar more than panic around a tiny \"anabolic window.\"\n\nGood sources: eggs, Greek yoghurt, chicken, beef, fish, sardines, whey, tofu, tempeh,\nlentils and legumes, dairy, meat and seafood.\n\n## Carbohydrates: refilling the fuel tank\n\nCarbohydrates refill muscle glycogen — the stored carbohydrate your muscles use during\ntraining. They support your next session's performance, your energy and mood, training\nvolume, recovery, muscle fullness and high-intensity capacity.\n\nA good recovery meal includes protein, carbohydrates, fluids, micronutrients and salt or\nelectrolytes if you've been sweating heavily. Examples: eggs + potatoes + fruit; Greek\nyoghurt + oats + berries; rice + chicken + vegetables; sardines + sourdough + salad; a\nprotein smoothie + banana + oats; a beef or tofu bowl with rice and vegetables.\n\nHydration matters too — aim for light-yellow urine most of the day, fluids around\ntraining, salt with meals, and electrolytes if you're sweating heavily.\n\n## Micronutrients, fats and recovery chemistry\n\nMacros provide structure and fuel. Micronutrients run the machinery: magnesium for\nmuscle and nervous-system function, zinc for immune function and repair, B vitamins for\nenergy pathways, vitamin D for bone, muscle and immune function, iron for oxygen\ntransport (test before supplementing), selenium for antioxidant and thyroid-related\nsystems, choline for brain, liver and cell membranes, and omega-3 fats for inflammatory\nbalance and recovery.\n\nSleep is biological recovery too — it regulates hormones, immune activity, repair, brain\nfunction and energy balance. Supplements can help in specific cases, but they never\nreplace enough food, protein, hydration and sleep.\n\n## What to remember\n\nAfter training, build a meal with 20–40 g protein, carbohydrates matched to the\nsession's intensity, micronutrients from whole foods, and water with salt or\nelectrolytes if needed.\n\nTrack energy, soreness, sleep, appetite, digestion and next-session performance.\n"},{"slug":"how-nutrition-training-gut-recovery-and-nervous-system-work-together","title":"How Nutrition, Training, Gut, Recovery and Nervous System Work Together","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# How Nutrition, Training, Gut, Recovery and Nervous System Work Together\n\nYour body does not separate nutrition, training, gut, recovery and nervous system into\ndifferent boxes — they work as one loop. Training creates the signal, nutrition supplies\nthe materials, your gut processes them, recovery turns signal into adaptation, and your\nnervous system controls output and regulation.\n\n## Training creates the signal\n\nDifferent kinds of training send different signals:\n\n- **Strength training** — mechanical tension, recruitment, muscle growth.\n- **Eccentric training** — control under load, force absorption, tissue capacity.\n- **Zone 2** — aerobic base, mitochondria, blood supply.\n- **Zones 4–5 / HIIT** — metabolic pressure, aerobic ceiling, recovery between efforts.\n- **ROM / loaded mobility** — range control, movement quality, recovery.\n\nTraining creates a request. The rest of the system determines whether your body can\nanswer it.\n\n## Nutrition and gut provide the materials\n\nFood supplies the building blocks: protein for amino acids, carbohydrates for glycogen\nand training fuel, fats for membranes and signalling, micronutrients for energy and\nrepair pathways, and hydration and electrolytes for circulation, muscle and nerve\nfunction.\n\nBut that food has to be digested, absorbed and tolerated first. The gut-performance link\ncovers nutrient absorption, energy availability, immune activation, inflammatory tone,\ndigestive comfort, appetite, microbiome metabolism and gut-brain communication.\n\nThe useful question is not only *is this food healthy?* — it is **does this food work for\nyour goal, your gut, your routine and your response?**\n\n## Recovery and nervous system turn effort into progress\n\nRecovery works on three layers:\n\n- **Biological** — protein, carbs, micronutrients, hydration, sleep.\n- **Physical** — walking, ROM, mobility, cooldowns, blood flow.\n- **Nervous system** — breathing, NSDR, Yoga Nidra, sleep rhythm, mental decompression.\n\nThe connected cycle: training creates the signal → nutrition provides materials → gut\ndigests and absorbs → recovery rebuilds → nervous system regulates activation and output\n→ sleep consolidates the adaptation → feedback tells you what happened → the plan\nadjusts.\n\nPerformance is built from this loop, not from any single lever pulled in isolation.\n\n## What to remember\n\nOnce a week, run the system check:\n\n- Training: did I give the right signal?\n- Nutrition: enough protein, carbs, fats and micros?\n- Gut: did I digest and tolerate meals well?\n- Recovery: did I sleep, hydrate, refuel and move gently?\n- Nervous system: did I downshift after effort?\n\nThen pick **one** adjustment — not ten.\n"},{"slug":"micro-workouts-exercise-snacks","title":"Micro-Workouts / Exercise Snacks","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Micro-Workouts / Exercise Snacks\n\nYou don't always need one long workout to create a useful signal. Micro-workouts — also\ncalled exercise snacks — are short, focused bouts of exercise distributed across the\nday. For your programme, we test 3 x 10 minutes across the day.\n\n## What micro-workouts are\n\nA micro-workout is a short training block, usually 5–15 minutes — not random movement,\nbut a small intentional dose of exercise. For example: 10 minutes in the morning, 10 in\nthe afternoon, 10 in the evening. That's 30 minutes of movement in a format that's much\neasier to integrate.\n\nMicro-workouts reduce friction. You don't need a perfect moment or a full hour — just one\nsmall block to start. The goal isn't to replace all structured training forever; it's to\ngive you another tool for consistency, energy and flexible implementation.\n\n## What happens in the body\n\nEvery short bout of movement increases blood flow, activates muscles, increases energy\ndemand and stimulates metabolic pathways. A 10-minute block can target a squat pattern,\npush pattern, pull pattern, hip hinge, core, loaded carry, mobility, a high-effort\ninterval or a breathing reset.\n\nFor muscle-building, micro-workouts still need enough mechanical tension and progressive\nchallenge — your muscle responds to tension, effort, volume, control and recovery, not\nonly to the format of the session. Micro-workouts are especially useful for consistency,\nenergy, movement quality and busy days; longer sessions may still be better for heavy\nlifting, skill work and progression.\n\n## How to use micro-workouts\n\nStart with an experiment: replace one training day with 3 x 10 minutes.\n\n- **Morning — activation block:** breathing reset, dynamic mobility, bodyweight or split\n  squats, push-ups or incline push-ups, light core.\n- **Afternoon — strength signal:** 2–3 movements — split squat, row, push-up, hinge or\n  carry — with good technique and controlled effort.\n- **Evening — mobility/recovery block:** hip mobility, ankle mobility, shoulder control,\n  loaded ROM, slow breathing.\n\n## What to remember\n\nReplace one normal training day with a morning activation block, an afternoon strength\nsignal, and an evening mobility/recovery block — 10 minutes each.\n\nTrack energy, mood, soreness, sleep, muscle feeling, motivation, and whether the format\nfelt repeatable.\n"},{"slug":"post-meal-walking","title":"Post-Meal Walking","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# Post-Meal Walking\n\nA short walk after eating is a simple metabolic habit. It helps your muscles use energy\nafter a meal, supports how your body handles post-meal glucose, and gives you useful\nfeedback on digestion and energy.\n\n## Why walking after meals matters\n\nAfter a meal, your body digests and absorbs nutrients — carbohydrates become glucose,\nprotein becomes amino acids, fats absorb more slowly, and hormones coordinate the\nprocess.\n\nWalking after eating activates your muscles, increases blood flow and helps move glucose\ninto working muscle. This isn't about fearing carbohydrates — they're useful for\ntraining and recovery. The goal is simply handling meals efficiently.\n\nTiming: usually 5–30 minutes after eating, adapted to your comfort. Duration: start with\n5–10 minutes.\n\n## What happens in the body\n\nMuscle contraction increases glucose uptake. Think of your muscles as a sponge: the meal\nprovides fuel, the walk activates tissue that can use or store it. The strongest\nevidence is around post-meal glucose management; for digestion and gut comfort, we lean\non your own feedback.\n\nWhat to watch for: less sleepiness after meals, less heaviness, better energy stability,\nless bloating for some people, more daily movement, a better step count, and an evening\ndownshift if it's after dinner. This one habit connects nutrition, gut work, glucose\nhandling and daily movement.\n\n## How to use post-meal walks\n\nStart with one main meal per day for two weeks. Good candidates: lunch, dinner, the meal\nthat makes you feel heaviest, your highest-carbohydrate meal, or the meal after which you\nusually sit for a long time.\n\nKeep intensity easy to moderate — no rushing, no stomach discomfort, no punishment\nmindset. Avoid turning it into high-intensity cardio, or obsessing over glucose-monitor\nnumbers if you're using one.\n\n## What to remember\n\nFor two weeks: walk after one main meal a day, 5–10 minutes, starting 5–30 minutes after\neating, at an easy pace.\n\nTrack energy, sleepiness, bloating, digestion, mood and step count.\n"},{"slug":"rom-and-loaded-mobility","title":"ROM & Loaded Mobility","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# ROM & Loaded Mobility\n\nFlexibility is the range you can reach. Mobility is the range you can control. Loaded\nmobility builds strength and control through range, instead of relying only on passive\nstretching.\n\n## Mobility is control\n\nFlexibility and mobility are related but not the same: **flexibility** means you can\nreach the position, **mobility** means you can control it, and **loaded mobility** means\nyou can produce force in it. If you can drop into a deep squat but can't control it,\nthat range may not help you much. If you can move into a squat, stay stable, breathe,\ncontrol your knees and produce force, the range becomes genuinely useful. The goal isn't\nextreme flexibility — it's usable movement capacity.\n\n## What ROM work does\n\nRange-of-motion training improves joint control, muscle-length tolerance, coordination,\ntissue capacity, strength in deeper positions, movement confidence and exercise quality.\nFull range-of-motion resistance training can be useful for lower-body strength and\nhypertrophy when the range is controlled and pain-free.\n\nCurrent hypertrophy research also looks at long-muscle-length training and lengthened\npartials. The key isn't forcing extreme positions — it's respecting controlled tension\nat longer muscle lengths.\n\n## How to use ROM and loaded mobility\n\nBefore training, keep ROM dynamic and easy — ankle rocks, hip circles, thoracic\nrotations, a light squat pattern, shoulder circles, scapular push-ups. After training,\nuse slower ROM to restore control — split squat position breathing, goblet squat prying,\nfull-range calf raises, Cossack variations, shoulder CARs. On recovery days, 10–15\nminutes light to moderate is enough.\n\nA first protocol: deep squat hold with movement (60 sec), split squat position breathing\n(45 sec/side), full-range calf raise lowering (8 slow reps/side), hip hinge reach (8\nreps), thoracic rotation (6 reps/side), shoulder controlled circles (5 each direction),\nslow nasal breathing (2 min).\n\n## What to remember\n\nAfter two training sessions this week, add 10 minutes of ROM / loaded mobility focused\non hips, ankles, squat position, hamstrings, shoulders and breathing.\n\nRules: no sharp pain, no forcing, slow control, comfortable breathing, finish feeling\nbetter than when you started.\n"},{"slug":"nutrient-dense-foods","title":"Nutrient-Dense Foods","tags":null,"body_md":"![Nutrient-Dense Foods — choose the foods that solve the nutritional gap you actually have](https://ndojytvvlvlbgtodujkf.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/content-public/library/nutrient-dense-foods/04_nutrient_dense_foods.png)\n\n## You found the gap. Now improve the food.\n\nOnce a repeated gap appears, the next step should be practical.\n\nNutrient density means choosing foods that provide a high amount of useful nutrition without needing to radically increase food volume or complexity.\n\nUseful foods may include, according to preference and tolerance:\n\n- eggs;\n- sardines;\n- oily fish;\n- quality dairy;\n- legumes;\n- nuts;\n- seeds;\n- colourful vegetables;\n- fruit;\n- shellfish or other zinc-rich foods;\n- mineral-rich whole foods.\n\n## The food-first logic\n\n```text\nIDENTIFY GAP\n↓\nCHOOSE A USEFUL FOOD\n↓\nREPEAT CONSISTENTLY\n↓\nREVIEW RESPONSE\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n### Eggs\nCan contribute:\n- choline;\n- B vitamins;\n- selenium;\n- protein.\n\n### Oily fish\nCan contribute:\n- omega-3;\n- vitamin D;\n- selenium;\n- protein.\n\n### Legumes\nCan contribute:\n- magnesium;\n- folate;\n- iron;\n- potassium;\n- fibre.\n\n### Nuts and seeds\nCan contribute:\n- magnesium;\n- zinc;\n- selenium depending on the food;\n- healthy fats.\n\n### Colourful plants\nCan contribute:\n- potassium;\n- folate;\n- vitamin C;\n- polyphenols;\n- fibre.\n\nFood choice should always be adapted to:\n\n- appetite;\n- culture;\n- schedule;\n- gut tolerance;\n- and the wider plan.\n\n## Key takeaway\n\n> **The goal is not to eat every “healthy” food. It is to choose the foods that solve the nutritional problem you actually have.**"},{"slug":"nsdr-yoga-nidra","title":"NSDR / Yoga Nidra","tags":null,"body_md":"\n# NSDR / Yoga Nidra\n\nRecovery isn't only muscular. Sometimes your nervous system is still switched on. NSDR\nand Yoga Nidra give your body a structured way to downshift without needing to fall\nasleep.\n\n## What NSDR and Yoga Nidra are\n\nNSDR means Non-Sleep Deep Rest — guided practices that help your body enter deep rest\nwhile awake or semi-awake. Yoga Nidra is a traditional guided lying-down practice using\nbreath, body awareness and relaxation. You don't need flexibility or meditation\nexperience — you lie down, close your eyes, listen and follow the guidance.\n\nNSDR is not a replacement for sleep. It's a recovery tool for moments when you're\nphysically tired, mentally wired, stressed, sleep-deprived or overloaded.\n\n## What happens in the body\n\nHard training and stress shift your body toward activation — higher heart rate,\nbreathing, tension, attention and vigilance. That activation is useful for performance,\nbut you also need the ability to come back down. NSDR and Yoga Nidra help create that\ndownshifting signal — possible benefits include stress reduction, relaxation, sleep\nquality, mood, perceived recovery and mental clarity. The evidence is promising, but\nshouldn't be exaggerated. Use it as a low-cost, practical reset tool.\n\n## How to use NSDR / Yoga Nidra\n\nLie down, put on headphones, choose a guided NSDR or Yoga Nidra audio, set an alarm if\nneeded, and follow the voice. If you fall asleep, that's fine. If your mind wanders, just\nreturn to the guidance.\n\nGood times to use it: after intense training, after poor sleep, during stressful days,\nas a midday reset, on recovery days, when you're wired but tired, or before evening if it\ndoesn't disrupt your sleep. Start with 10–20 minutes, 2–3 times per week.\n\n## What to remember\n\nFor two weeks: 2–3 NSDR/Yoga Nidra sessions per week, 10–20 minutes each, at the times\nthat suit you best — after training, midday, on a recovery day, after poor sleep or\nmental overload.\n\nTrack calm, energy, clarity, sleep, mood, readiness and next-day recovery.\n"}]
